course: geography
lesson: 60
title: Metals, mining and what they cost
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Almost every metal in your house began as a rock. Rocks with enough metal in them to be worth digging are called ores, and smelting heats the ore with a fuel such as charcoal or coke to free the metal.
explain: Long ago people learned to mix copper with tin to make bronze, hard enough for tools and swords, and tin from Cornwall was traded across Europe for thousands of years. Later iron took over; with a little carbon it becomes steel, the most used metal material of all.
explain: Your phone holds dozens of metals, among them lithium, cobalt, copper and rare earth elements. To get them, open-pit mines dig holes more than a kilometre deep and leave mountains of waste rock, and some mines poison rivers.
explain: Mining has a human cost too: Indigenous people were forced to dig silver in Peru and Bolivia, enslaved Africans dug gold in Brazil, and today children have been found working in some small cobalt mines in the Congo. Recycling metals uses far less energy than mining new ore.
fact: Metals usually come from ores, rocks containing enough metal to be worth mining.
fact: Smelting heats ore with fuel such as charcoal or coke (a fuel made from coal) to free the metal.
fact: Bronze is copper mixed with tin, and Cornish tin was traded across Europe for thousands of years.
fact: Iron is the most used metal, and steel is iron with a little carbon, making it stronger.
fact: Phones and electric cars need dozens of metals, including lithium, cobalt, copper and rare earth elements.
fact: Open-pit mines dig huge holes, some over a kilometre deep, and leave mountains of waste rock.
fact: Mining can poison rivers with acid and toxic metals such as mercury and arsenic.
fact: Some cobalt in batteries comes from small mines in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, where children have been found working.
fact: European colonisers forced Indigenous people to mine silver in Peru and Bolivia, and enslaved Africans to dig gold in Brazil.
fact: Mines today are often on Indigenous land, and in 2020 a mining company blew up the Juukan Gorge rock shelters in Australia, used by Aboriginal people for 46,000 years.
fact: Recycling metals uses much less energy than mining new ore, and aluminium can be recycled again and again.
exercise: What is an ore? | A rock with enough metal to be worth mining | Any shiny stone you pick up on a beach or riverbank | A tool used by miners | A kind of fossil fuel
exercise: What does smelting do? | Heats ore with fuel to free the metal | Freezes ore hard so that it cracks open | Washes ore in the sea | Grinds metal into fine sand
exercise: Which fuels have been used for smelting? | Charcoal or coke | Ice or packed snow | Sand or rock salt | Wool or cotton cloth
exercise: What is bronze? | Copper mixed with tin | Iron mixed with pure gold | Silver mixed with salt | Pure copper and nothing else
exercise: Which part of Britain was famous for tin traded across Europe? | Cornwall | Kent and Sussex | The Shetland Islands | London's docks
exercise: What is steel? | Iron with a little carbon, making it stronger | Copper with a little tin | Pure iron with no carbon at all, which makes it the hardest | Aluminium mixed with glass
exercise: Which metal is used the most? | Iron | Pure gold | Platinum | Silver
exercise: Which metals do phones and electric cars need? | Lithium, cobalt, copper and rare earth elements | Only gold and silver, which carry electricity best | Only iron | Only tin and lead
exercise: What is an open-pit mine? | A huge hole dug in the ground, some over a kilometre deep | A long tunnel dug deep under the sea bed to reach the coal below | A small hole for one person | A cave made by rain
exercise: What can mining do to rivers? | Poison them with acid, mercury and arsenic | Make them cleaner and clearer than they were before | Turn them into glaciers | Make them flow uphill
exercise: In which country have children been found working in small cobalt mines? | The Democratic Republic of the Congo | Iceland | Switzerland | New Zealand
exercise: Who was forced to dig gold in Brazil? | Enslaved Africans | Free Swiss engineers | Roman soldiers | Chinese astronauts
exercise: Where did colonisers force Indigenous people to mine silver? | Peru and Bolivia | Norway and Sweden | Japan and Korea | Wales and Ireland
exercise: What happened at Juukan Gorge in Australia in 2020? | A mining company blew up 46,000-year-old rock shelters | A new gold mine was opened there by its Aboriginal owners | A glacier carved a new valley | Scientists found the oldest diamond
exercise: Why is recycling metal better than mining new ore? | It uses much less energy | It makes the metal radioactive | It needs more mines | It turns metal into plastic
exercise: Which metal can be recycled again and again? | Aluminium | Coal | Oil | Peat
test: Ella reckons metals just come out of the ground shiny and ready. right? | No, most come from ores that must be smelted | Yes, all metals are dug up pure and shiny | Yes, metals grow like crystals in shops until they are sold | No, metals are made from wood
test: Ancient Greeks wanted bronze but had copper and no tin. What would they need to do? | Trade for tin from places like Cornwall | Melt sand into tin | Use salt instead of tin | Just use copper on its own and call it bronze anyway
test: Your old phone sits in a drawer. Why might recycling it help? | Its metals can be reused without mining new ore | Phones turn back into rock if left in drawers | Old phones make more lithium on their own over time | Recycling makes the phone radioactive
test: "electric cars r totally clean, no mining at all" — Max. What would you add? | They need mined metals like lithium and cobalt | Electric cars are made of wood | Electric cars run on coal only, burnt in the boot | Nothing, no metals are needed
test: A river below an old mine runs orange and fish have died. What most likely happened? | Acid and toxic metals from the mine poisoned it | The fish turned the water orange | Rain makes rivers orange every year in the autumn | The sun dried the river
test: A game shows a happy cartoon mine where workers in Potosí sing on payday. What is the true history? | Indigenous people were forced to mine silver and many died | They were well-paid volunteers who came from all over the world | No one ever worked there | Machines did all the work
test: What would happen to a blacksmith's iron sword if he added a little carbon to make steel? | It would become stronger | It would turn into gold | It would melt at room temperature | It would rust away at once
test: Why did people say the destruction of Juukan Gorge was a terrible loss? | It held 46,000 years of Aboriginal history | It was a car park | It was an empty quarry | It was a brand new shopping centre with lots of shops
test: shop sells "ethical" phones — why might people check where the cobalt came from? | Some cobalt comes from mines where children have been found working | Cobalt from some places is fake and makes the phones break after a month | Cobalt makes phones too heavy | Cobalt is only used in toys
test: A giant open-pit mine is visible from space. What does it leave behind besides the hole? | Mountains of waste rock | A new forest | A clean lake of drinking water | Nothing at all
test: Why do aluminium can recycling bins matter? | Aluminium can be recycled again and again with much less energy | Cans turn into oil when recycled | Aluminium cannot be recycled at all | Recycling cans uses more energy than mining
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an ore? | A rock containing a quantity of metal sufficient to warrant extraction | Any lustrous mineral discovered upon a shore | An implement employed by miners | A variety of fossil fuel
exam: Identify the composition of the alloy designated bronze. | Copper combined with tin | Iron combined with carbon | Silver combined with gold | Aluminium combined with lead
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the environmental consequences of mining. | Rivers may be contaminated with acid and toxic metals such as mercury and arsenic | Rivers are invariably purified by mining activity | Mining produces no waste rock whatsoever | Mining increases the fertility of adjacent soils
exam: Ascertain the principal advantage of the recycling of metals in respect of energy. | Considerably less energy is consumed than in the mining of new ore | Considerably more energy is consumed than in mining | No energy whatsoever is consumed | Energy consumption is identical in both cases
